    "result_text": "Tests are green and the emitted event is unchanged. \n\n## Summary\n\nI agree with both findings \u2014 the JSON concern didn't belong in the controller. Refactored:\n\n**New `audit/OwnerAuditEvents.java`** (`@Component`) \u2014 a dedicated unit that owns the cohesive \"structured event\" concern:\n- The monotonic `seq` counter (`AtomicLong`).\n- The `OwnerCreatedEvent` record defining the payload shape `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}`.\n- Serialization via the injected Jackson `ObjectMapper` (the standard path this codebase already uses), replacing the hand-rolled escaper entirely.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 now injects `OwnerAuditEvents` and calls `ownerAuditEvents.ownerCreated(owner.getId(), owner.primaryIdentifier(), ...)`. Deleted `ownerCreatedEvent()`, `jsonString()`, the `eventSeq` field, and the `AtomicLong` import.\n\n**`Owner.primaryIdentifier()`** \u2014 left as-is, as the review recommended; the migration design was called out as sound.\n\nThe emitted event is byte-for-byte identical (`{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`), acceptance behaviour is intact, and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne note: Jackson serializes record components in declaration order, so field order is now guaranteed by the record definition rather than a format string \u2014 the acceptance test asserts on substring containment (marker, id, identifier) so it doesn't depend on ordering either way.",
