        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe `primaryIdentifier()` abstraction on `Owner` is the clean part of this change: it's a domain method on the entity, it captures \"whatever identifies the owner right now,\" and it lets the migration to `memberId` happen without touching the call site. That belongs exactly where it is. The problem is on the controller side.\n\n**1. Hand-rolled JSON serialization doesn't belong in a Spring Boot REST controller.**\nThis app already has Jackson on the classpath (it's a Spring MVC REST app serializing DTOs). Building the event with `String.format` plus a bespoke `jsonString()` escaper \u2014 quote/backslash/`\\n`/`\\r`/`\\t` handling and a `\\uXXXX` fallback loop \u2014 reimplements what `ObjectMapper` does correctly and idiomatically. A maintainer here would not expect to find a general-purpose JSON string escaper living inside `OwnerRestControllerV1`. Prefer a small event type serialized via Jackson, e.g.:\n\n```java\nrecord OwnerCreatedEvent(long seq, Integer ownerId, String customerCode,\n                         Integer membershipLevel, String event) {}\n// ...\nobjectMapper.writeValueAsString(new OwnerCreatedEvent(\n    eventSeq.incrementAndGet(), owner.getId(), owner.primaryIdentifier(),\n    ownerDto.getMembershipLevel(), \"OWNER_CREATED\"));\n```\n\nThat deletes `jsonString()` entirely and removes the manual field-ordering/escaping burden.\n\n**2. The controller is accreting responsibilities that aren't its job.**\n`jsonString()` is a generic serialization utility with nothing to do with request handling \u2014 this is the controller drifting toward a catch-all. Even if you keep manual formatting, the event's construction and rendering (the `seq` counter, the shape of the payload, the serialization) is a cohesive concern that would sit more naturally in its own unit (an event type / small audit-event component) rather than as private static helpers on the REST controller. Keeping it here means the next structured event added will pile more serialization code into the same class.\n\nNeither finding is about correctness or the migration design \u2014 that part is sound. It's about moving the JSON concern out of the controller and onto the standard Jackson path this codebase already uses."
